  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 PM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES-East Day Cloud Phase RGB imagery shows some \nisolated high cirrus, a couple patches of stubborn marine fog, and a \nfair weather cu patch in Menominee County but otherwise clear \nconditions across the region. The 500mb pattern resembles an omega \ndirectly over the Upper Great Lakes with the peak of the ridge over \nLake Superior and deep troughs over the East Coast and over the High \nPlains vertically stacked upon their respective surface features. \nThis pattern will gradually break down over the next few days, \nbeginning with the approach of the trough from the Plains tonight. \n\nCAMs show a swath of scattered light rain showers approaching from \nthe west overnight. The absolute earliest that the HREF shows \nshowers arriving near Ironwood is 08Z, though as the showers push \neast, they decrease in coverage until being almost entirely \ndissipated by noon. Chances of any location getting measurable \nrainfall totals are only around 50 percent, higher in the far west, \nlower to the east. The rain is not going to be impactful either, as \nthe only areas with greater than 25% chances of a tenth of an inch \nof rain are west of Marenisco/Lake Gogebic/M-64, and with that \nentire area in D3 Extreme Drought, it would take more rain than that \nto see meaningful relief. Elsewhere in the UP, light winds and clear \nskies in the east half overnight will lead to radiation fog chances, \nwhich per the HREF are around 40-60 percent. \n\nModels are somewhat split on if light rain shower chances linger \nSunday night into Monday, though the NBM keeps it a dry solution. By \n12Z Monday, the 500mb pattern shows a ridge axis that extends from \nwest TX to the UP bounded to the east by a closed low over New \nJersey and to the west by a closed low over central California and \nHurricane Polo, in addition to a shortwave over the Dakotas and \nCanadian Prairie. These western troughs will help kick the ridge \nquickly east, though not before quiet weather through at least \nMonday night.\n\nHurricane Polo and the California closed low will merge over the \nFour Corners by Wednesday morning as the Plains shortwave moves \nthrough the region. The LREF shows 15-30% chances of precip entering \nthe west as soon as 18Z Tuesday with widespread 50-70% chances of \nprecip by 06Z Wednesday. A few rumbles of thunder can't even be \nruled out as Euro ensemble mean MUCAPE 00Z Wednesday is up to 200 \nJ/kg. This looks to be the best chance at widespread soaking rain in \nsome time as LREF chances of a quarter inch of rain per 24 hours is \nover 40% for most of the UP at some point Tuesday through Thursday, \nthough there is enough spread in the details that a more precise \nforecast is uncertain, especially late in the week as ensembles \nstruggle to agree on how to resolve the fate of the Four Corners low.",
